Top 20 Java 8 Interview Questions (Part 1: Questions 1-10)
1. What are the important features introduced in Java 8?
Answer

Java 8 introduced several revolutionary features that brought functional programming capabilities to Java.

Features
Lambda Expressions
Functional Interfaces
Stream API
Method References
Default Methods
Static Methods in Interfaces
Optional Class
Date and Time API
Parallel Streams
Nashorn JavaScript Engine

Interview Answer

"Java 8 introduced Lambda Expressions, Stream API, Functional Interfaces, Method References, Optional Class, Default Methods, and the new Date-Time API, making Java more concise and functional."

2. What is a Lambda Expression?
Answer

A lambda expression is an anonymous function used to implement functional interfaces.

Syntax
(parameters) -> expression

Example

Before Java 8

Runnable r = new Runnable() {
    @Override
    public void run() {
        System.out.println("Hello");
    }
};


After Java 8

Runnable r = () -> System.out.println("Hello");

Advantages
Less Code
Better Readability
Functional Programming Support

Interview Answer

"A Lambda Expression is an anonymous function that provides a concise way to implement functional interfaces."

3. What is a Functional Interface?
Answer

A Functional Interface contains exactly one abstract method.

Examples
Runnable
Callable
Comparator
Predicate
Function
Consumer
Supplier

Custom Functional Interface
@FunctionalInterface
interface Calculator {
    int add(int a, int b);
}

Lambda Usage
Calculator c = (a,b) -> a+b;

Interview Answer

"A Functional Interface contains exactly one abstract method and can be implemented using lambda expressions."

4. What is @FunctionalInterface Annotation?
Answer

This annotation indicates that the interface is intended to be a Functional Interface.

Example
@FunctionalInterface
interface Test {
    void display();
}

Benefits
Compile-time Validation
Better Readability
Prevents Extra Abstract Methods

Interview Answer

"@FunctionalInterface ensures the interface contains only one abstract method and provides compile-time validation."

5. What are predefined Functional Interfaces in Java 8?
Answer

Java 8 introduced interfaces under:

java.util.function

Most Important Interfaces
Predicate<T>
Function<T,R>
Consumer<T>
Supplier<T>
UnaryOperator<T>
BinaryOperator<T>

Examples

Predicate

Predicate<Integer> even = n -> n%2==0;


Function

Function<String,Integer> f = String::length;


Consumer

Consumer<String> c = System.out::println;


Supplier

Supplier<String> s = () -> "Hello";

Interview Answer

"Predicate, Function, Consumer, and Supplier are the most commonly used predefined functional interfaces in Java 8."

6. What is Stream API?
Answer

Stream API is used to process collections in a functional style.

Example
List<String> names =
Arrays.asList("John","Mike","Tom");

names.stream()
     .filter(n -> n.startsWith("J"))
     .forEach(System.out::println);

Output
John

Advantages
Less Code
Lazy Evaluation
Parallel Processing
Improved Readability

Interview Answer

"Stream API provides a declarative way to process collections using operations such as filter, map, reduce, and collect."

7. Difference Between Collection and Stream?
Collection
Stores Data
Eager Processing

Stream
Processes Data
Lazy Processing

Example

Collection

list.add("A");


Stream

list.stream()
    .filter(x -> x.startsWith("A"));

Interview Answer

"A Collection stores data, whereas a Stream processes data without modifying the original collection."

8. What is Lazy Evaluation in Streams?
Answer

Intermediate operations are not executed until a terminal operation is called.

Example
Stream.of(1,2,3,4)
      .filter(n -> {
          System.out.println(n);
          return n%2==0;
      });


Output

No Output


Because terminal operation is missing.

Correct
Stream.of(1,2,3,4)
      .filter(n -> n%2==0)
      .count();

Interview Answer

"Streams use lazy evaluation, meaning operations execute only when a terminal operation is invoked."

9. What are Intermediate and Terminal Operations?
Intermediate Operations

Return Stream.

Examples:

filter()
map()
sorted()
distinct()
limit()
skip()

Terminal Operations

Return Result.

Examples:

collect()
count()
forEach()
reduce()
findFirst()

Example
list.stream()
    .filter(x -> x>10)
    .map(x -> x*2)
    .collect(Collectors.toList());

Interview Answer

"Intermediate operations produce another stream, whereas terminal operations produce the final result."

10. What is map() in Stream API?
Answer

map() transforms each element into another form.

Example
List<String> names =
Arrays.asList("john","mike");

List<String> upper =
names.stream()
     .map(String::toUpperCase)
     .collect(Collectors.toList());

Output
[JOHN, MIKE]

Real Banking Example

Convert Account Entities into DTOs.

accounts.stream()
        .map(AccountDTO::new)
        .toList();

Interview Answer

"map() transforms each stream element into another object while preserving the number of elements."

Java 8 Revision Cheat Sheet
Lambda               → Anonymous Function

Functional Interface → One Abstract Method

@FunctionalInterface → Compile-Time Validation

Predicate            → Input → Boolean

Function             → Input → Output

Consumer             → Input, No Output

Supplier             → No Input, Output

Stream               → Functional Collection Processing

Intermediate Ops     → filter, map, sorted

Terminal Ops         → collect, count, reduce

map()                → Transform Data


